#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186556226 :question/problem "Simplify the trigonometric expression $\\sin \\theta(4\\cos \\theta-6\\sin \\theta)-6\\cos^{2}\\theta.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we expand the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\sin \\theta(4\\cos \\theta-6\\sin \\theta)-6\\cos^{2}\\theta = 4\\sin \\theta\\cos \\theta-6\\sin^{2}\\theta-6\\cos^{2}\\theta\n$$\n\nNext, we group the $\\sin^2 \\theta$ and $\\cos^2 \\theta$ terms.\n\n$$\n4\\sin \\theta\\cos \\theta-6\\sin^{2}\\theta-6\\cos^{2}\\theta = 4\\sin \\theta\\cos \\theta-6(\\sin^{2}\\theta+\\cos^{2}\\theta)\n$$\n\nThen, we use $\\sin^2{\\theta}+\\cos^2{\\theta}=1$ to simplify the expression in the parentheses:\n\n$$\n4\\sin \\theta\\cos \\theta-6(\\sin^{2}\\theta+\\cos^{2}\\theta) = 4 \\sin \\theta \\cos \\theta - 6\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\sin \\theta(4\\cos \\theta-6\\sin \\theta)-6\\cos^{2}\\theta = 4 \\sin \\theta \\cos \\theta - 6.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186556236 :question/problem "Simplify the trigonometric expression $(4\\sin \\theta-4\\cos \\theta)^{2}+(4\\sin \\theta+4\\cos \\theta)^{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we expand the parentheses:\n\n$$\n(4\\sin \\theta-4\\cos \\theta)^{2}+(4\\sin \\theta+4\\cos \\theta)^{2} = 16\\sin^{2}\\theta-32\\sin \\theta\\cos \\theta+16\\cos^{2}\\theta+16\\sin^{2}\\theta+32\\sin \\theta\\cos \\theta+16\\cos^{2}\\theta\n$$\n\nNext, we group the $\\sin^2 \\theta$ and $\\cos^2 \\theta$ terms.\n\n$$\n16\\sin^{2}\\theta-32\\sin \\theta\\cos \\theta+16\\cos^{2}\\theta+16\\sin^{2}\\theta+32\\sin \\theta\\cos \\theta+16\\cos^{2}\\theta = 32(\\sin^{2}\\theta+\\cos^{2}\\theta)\n$$\n\nThen, we use $\\sin^2{\\theta}+\\cos^2{\\theta}=1$ to simplify the expression in the parentheses:\n\n$$\n32(\\sin^{2}\\theta+\\cos^{2}\\theta) = 32\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n(4\\sin \\theta-4\\cos \\theta)^{2}+(4\\sin \\theta+4\\cos \\theta)^{2} = 32.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186556250 :question/problem "Simplify the trigonometric expression $\\sin \\theta(3\\cos \\theta-3\\sin \\theta)+\\cos \\theta(6\\sin \\theta-3\\cos \\theta).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we expand the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\sin \\theta(3\\cos \\theta-3\\sin \\theta)+\\cos \\theta(6\\sin \\theta-3\\cos \\theta) = 3\\sin \\theta\\cos \\theta-3\\sin^{2}\\theta+6\\sin \\theta\\cos \\theta-3\\cos^{2}\\theta\n$$\n\nNext, we group the $\\sin^2 \\theta$ and $\\cos^2 \\theta$ terms.\n\n$$\n3\\sin \\theta\\cos \\theta-3\\sin^{2}\\theta+6\\sin \\theta\\cos \\theta-3\\cos^{2}\\theta = 9\\sin \\theta\\cos \\theta-3(\\sin^{2}\\theta+\\cos^{2}\\theta)\n$$\n\nThen, we use $\\sin^2{\\theta}+\\cos^2{\\theta}=1$ to simplify the expression in the parentheses:\n\n$$\n9\\sin \\theta\\cos \\theta-3(\\sin^{2}\\theta+\\cos^{2}\\theta) = 9 \\sin \\theta \\cos \\theta - 3\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\sin \\theta(3\\cos \\theta-3\\sin \\theta)+\\cos \\theta(6\\sin \\theta-3\\cos \\theta) = 9 \\sin \\theta \\cos \\theta - 3.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186556253 :question/problem "Simplify the trigonometric expression $\\sin \\theta(5\\cos \\theta-4\\sin \\theta)+\\cos \\theta(4\\sin \\theta-4\\cos \\theta).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we expand the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\sin \\theta(5\\cos \\theta-4\\sin \\theta)+\\cos \\theta(4\\sin \\theta-4\\cos \\theta) = 5\\sin \\theta\\cos \\theta-4\\sin^{2}\\theta+4\\sin \\theta\\cos \\theta-4\\cos^{2}\\theta\n$$\n\nNext, we group the $\\sin^2 \\theta$ and $\\cos^2 \\theta$ terms.\n\n$$\n5\\sin \\theta\\cos \\theta-4\\sin^{2}\\theta+4\\sin \\theta\\cos \\theta-4\\cos^{2}\\theta = 9\\sin \\theta\\cos \\theta-4(\\sin^{2}\\theta+\\cos^{2}\\theta)\n$$\n\nThen, we use $\\sin^2{\\theta}+\\cos^2{\\theta}=1$ to simplify the expression in the parentheses:\n\n$$\n9\\sin \\theta\\cos \\theta-4(\\sin^{2}\\theta+\\cos^{2}\\theta) = 9 \\sin \\theta \\cos \\theta - 4\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\sin \\theta(5\\cos \\theta-4\\sin \\theta)+\\cos \\theta(4\\sin \\theta-4\\cos \\theta) = 9 \\sin \\theta \\cos \\theta - 4.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186556256 :question/problem "Simplify the trigonometric expression $\\sin \\theta(-2\\cos \\theta-5\\sin \\theta)+\\cos \\theta(-4\\sin \\theta-5\\cos \\theta).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we expand the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\sin \\theta(-2\\cos \\theta-5\\sin \\theta)+\\cos \\theta(-4\\sin \\theta-5\\cos \\theta) = -2\\sin \\theta\\cos \\theta-5\\sin^{2}\\theta-4\\sin \\theta\\cos \\theta-5\\cos^{2}\\theta\n$$\n\nNext, we group the $\\sin^2 \\theta$ and $\\cos^2 \\theta$ terms.\n\n$$\n-2\\sin \\theta\\cos \\theta-5\\sin^{2}\\theta-4\\sin \\theta\\cos \\theta-5\\cos^{2}\\theta = -6\\sin \\theta\\cos \\theta-5(\\sin^{2}\\theta+\\cos^{2}\\theta)\n$$\n\nThen, we use $\\sin^2{\\theta}+\\cos^2{\\theta}=1$ to simplify the expression in the parentheses:\n\n$$\n-6\\sin \\theta\\cos \\theta-5(\\sin^{2}\\theta+\\cos^{2}\\theta) = - 6 \\sin \\theta \\cos \\theta - 5\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\sin \\theta(-2\\cos \\theta-5\\sin \\theta)+\\cos \\theta(-4\\sin \\theta-5\\cos \\theta) = - 6 \\sin \\theta \\cos \\theta - 5.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186556259 :question/problem "Simplify the trigonometric expression $\\sin \\theta(-8\\cos \\theta-5\\sin \\theta)-5\\cos^{2}\\theta.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we expand the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\sin \\theta(-8\\cos \\theta-5\\sin \\theta)-5\\cos^{2}\\theta = -8\\sin \\theta\\cos \\theta-5\\sin^{2}\\theta-5\\cos^{2}\\theta\n$$\n\nNext, we group the $\\sin^2 \\theta$ and $\\cos^2 \\theta$ terms.\n\n$$\n-8\\sin \\theta\\cos \\theta-5\\sin^{2}\\theta-5\\cos^{2}\\theta = -8\\sin \\theta\\cos \\theta-5(\\sin^{2}\\theta+\\cos^{2}\\theta)\n$$\n\nThen, we use $\\sin^2{\\theta}+\\cos^2{\\theta}=1$ to simplify the expression in the parentheses:\n\n$$\n-8\\sin \\theta\\cos \\theta-5(\\sin^{2}\\theta+\\cos^{2}\\theta) = - 8 \\sin \\theta \\cos \\theta - 5\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\sin \\theta(-8\\cos \\theta-5\\sin \\theta)-5\\cos^{2}\\theta = - 8 \\sin \\theta \\cos \\theta - 5.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186556272 :question/problem "Simplify the trigonometric expression $\\sin x(3\\cos x-2\\sin x)-2\\cos^{2}x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we expand the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\sin x(3\\cos x-2\\sin x)-2\\cos^{2}x = 3\\sin x\\cos x-2\\sin^{2}x-2\\cos^{2}x\n$$\n\nNext, we group the $\\sin^2 x$ and $\\cos^2 x$ terms.\n\n$$\n3\\sin x\\cos x-2\\sin^{2}x-2\\cos^{2}x = 3\\sin x\\cos x-2(\\sin^{2}x+\\cos^{2}x)\n$$\n\nThen, we use $\\sin^2{x}+\\cos^2{x}=1$ to simplify the expression in the parentheses:\n\n$$\n3\\sin x\\cos x-2(\\sin^{2}x+\\cos^{2}x) = 3 \\sin x \\cos x - 2\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\sin x(3\\cos x-2\\sin x)-2\\cos^{2}x = 3 \\sin x \\cos x - 2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186556280 :question/problem "Simplify the trigonometric expression $\\sin \\theta(8\\cos \\theta-3\\sin \\theta)-3\\cos^{2}\\theta.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we expand the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\sin \\theta(8\\cos \\theta-3\\sin \\theta)-3\\cos^{2}\\theta = 8\\sin \\theta\\cos \\theta-3\\sin^{2}\\theta-3\\cos^{2}\\theta\n$$\n\nNext, we group the $\\sin^2 \\theta$ and $\\cos^2 \\theta$ terms.\n\n$$\n8\\sin \\theta\\cos \\theta-3\\sin^{2}\\theta-3\\cos^{2}\\theta = 8\\sin \\theta\\cos \\theta-3(\\sin^{2}\\theta+\\cos^{2}\\theta)\n$$\n\nThen, we use $\\sin^2{\\theta}+\\cos^2{\\theta}=1$ to simplify the expression in the parentheses:\n\n$$\n8\\sin \\theta\\cos \\theta-3(\\sin^{2}\\theta+\\cos^{2}\\theta) = 8 \\sin \\theta \\cos \\theta - 3\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\sin \\theta(8\\cos \\theta-3\\sin \\theta)-3\\cos^{2}\\theta = 8 \\sin \\theta \\cos \\theta - 3.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186556285 :question/problem "Simplify the trigonometric expression $\\sin x(3\\cos x-2\\sin x)+\\cos x(3\\sin x-2\\cos x).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we expand the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\sin x(3\\cos x-2\\sin x)+\\cos x(3\\sin x-2\\cos x) = 3\\sin x\\cos x-2\\sin^{2}x+3\\sin x\\cos x-2\\cos^{2}x\n$$\n\nNext, we group the $\\sin^2 x$ and $\\cos^2 x$ terms.\n\n$$\n3\\sin x\\cos x-2\\sin^{2}x+3\\sin x\\cos x-2\\cos^{2}x = 6\\sin x\\cos x-2(\\sin^{2}x+\\cos^{2}x)\n$$\n\nThen, we use $\\sin^2{x}+\\cos^2{x}=1$ to simplify the expression in the parentheses:\n\n$$\n6\\sin x\\cos x-2(\\sin^{2}x+\\cos^{2}x) = 6 \\sin x \\cos x - 2\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\sin x(3\\cos x-2\\sin x)+\\cos x(3\\sin x-2\\cos x) = 6 \\sin x \\cos x - 2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186556292 :question/problem "The region $R$ is enclosed inside the cylinder ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=17$ between the surfaces $z=0$ and $z=12.$ Express the following triple integral in cylindrical polar coordinates. \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}{z}^{2}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}V\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/99/994f3537bf54a849e6a725c4c692a2326266d8065a742b1988308c793708827f.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is a right vertical cylinder of radius $\\sqrt{17}$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=0,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=12.$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta ,z)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le \\sqrt{17},\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,\\,0\\le z\\le 12\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}{z}^{2}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}{z}^{2}\\sqrt{(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}}\\cdot r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}r{z}^{2}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{17}}{\\int }_{0}^{12}{r}^{2}{z}^{2}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186556296 :question/problem "The region $R$ is enclosed inside the cylinder $x^2+y^2=5$ for $x\\le 0$ and $y\\ge 0$, between the surfaces $z=x$ and $z=y^{2} + 4$. Express the following triple integral in cylindrical polar coordinates.\n\n$$\n\\iiint_R z \\left(x^{2} - y^{2}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}V\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/3e/3e51a479b8fe2a00f7b51a8613fe2cb6a45d790d737637c31b7858766e0491ff.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is one-quarter of a right vertical cylinder of radius $\\sqrt{5}$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=x=r \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)},$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=y^{2} + 4=r^{2} \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 4.$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta,z):0\\le r\\le \\sqrt{5},\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\le\\theta\\le \\pi,r \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\le z\\le r^{2} \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 4\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iiint_R z \\left(x^{2} - y^{2}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}V&=\\iiint_\\Delta z((r\\cos\\theta)^2-(r\\sin\\theta)^2)\\cdot r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\iiint_\\Delta rz(r^2\\cos^2\\theta-r^2\\sin^2\\theta)\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\iiint_\\Delta r^3z(\\cos^2\\theta-\\sin^2\\theta)\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\iiint_\\Delta r^3z\\cos(2\\theta)\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &={\\int }_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\pi}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{5}}{\\int }_{r \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}^{r^{2} \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 4}r^{3} z \\cos{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186556300 :question/problem "The region $R$ is enclosed inside the cylinder $x^2+y^2=4$ for $x\\le 0$ and $y\\ge 0$, between the surfaces $z=x$ and $z=y^{2} + 12$. Express the following triple integral in cylindrical polar coordinates.\n\n$$\n\\iiint_R z \\left(x^{2} - y^{2}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}V\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/d8/d8c1c025e6e8992826d8629a129d83906a4af3c566d44dbb25fcdf7dca9df64d.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is one-quarter of a right vertical cylinder of radius $2$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=x=r \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)},$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=y^{2} + 12=r^{2} \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 12.$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta,z):0\\le r\\le 2,\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\le\\theta\\le \\pi,r \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\le z\\le r^{2} \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 12\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iiint_R z \\left(x^{2} - y^{2}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}V&=\\iiint_\\Delta z((r\\cos\\theta)^2-(r\\sin\\theta)^2)\\cdot r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\iiint_\\Delta rz(r^2\\cos^2\\theta-r^2\\sin^2\\theta)\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\iiint_\\Delta r^3z(\\cos^2\\theta-\\sin^2\\theta)\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\iiint_\\Delta r^3z\\cos(2\\theta)\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &={\\int }_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\pi}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{r \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}^{r^{2} \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 12}r^{3} z \\cos{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186556301 :question/problem "The region $R$ is enclosed inside the cylinder $x^2+y^2=30$ for $x\\le 0$ and $y\\ge 0$, between the surfaces $z=x$ and $z=y^{2} + 1$. Express the following triple integral in cylindrical polar coordinates.\n\n$$\n\\iiint_R z \\left(x^{2} - y^{2}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}V\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/07/07f1c4ca9e71cac2149bee4c4ae2e4f506f280b580ff0a391229f7e1624f2119.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is one-quarter of a right vertical cylinder of radius $\\sqrt{30}$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=x=r \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)},$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=y^{2} + 1=r^{2} \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 1.$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta,z):0\\le r\\le \\sqrt{30},\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\le\\theta\\le \\pi,r \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\le z\\le r^{2} \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 1\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iiint_R z \\left(x^{2} - y^{2}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}V&=\\iiint_\\Delta z((r\\cos\\theta)^2-(r\\sin\\theta)^2)\\cdot r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\iiint_\\Delta rz(r^2\\cos^2\\theta-r^2\\sin^2\\theta)\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\iiint_\\Delta r^3z(\\cos^2\\theta-\\sin^2\\theta)\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\iiint_\\Delta r^3z\\cos(2\\theta)\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &={\\int }_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\pi}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{30}}{\\int }_{r \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}^{r^{2} \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 1}r^{3} z \\cos{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186556306 :question/problem "The region $R$ is enclosed inside the cylinder ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=17$ for $x\\le 0,$ between the surfaces $z=x$ and $z={y}^{2}.$ Express the following triple integral in cylindrical polar coordinates. \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}V\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/24/249116dd79140b8e49d64902c46a3f0bc7c6411c9f8e7129faf38c64e7cc859e.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is one-half of a right vertical cylinder of radius $\\sqrt{17}$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z={y}^{2}={r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta ,z)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le \\sqrt{17},\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta <\\frac{3\\pi }{2},\\,r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\le z\\le {r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}((r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}-(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2})\\cdot r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}r({r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}{r}^{3}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{17}}{\\int }_{r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }^{{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186556308 :question/problem "The region $R$ is enclosed inside the cylinder ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=5$ for $x\\le 0$ and $y\\ge 0,$ between the surfaces $z=0$ and $z=\\sqrt{81-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}.$ Express the following triple integral in cylindrical polar coordinates. \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}\\text{d}V\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/3e/3e51a479b8fe2a00f7b51a8613fe2cb6a45d790d737637c31b7858766e0491ff.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is one-quarter of a right vertical cylinder of radius $\\sqrt{5}$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=0,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=\\sqrt{81-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}=\\sqrt{81-{r}^{2}}.$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta ,z)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le \\sqrt{5},\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\pi ,\\,0\\le z\\le \\sqrt{81-{r}^{2}}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}\\text{d}V & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{5}}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{81-{r}^{2}}}r\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186556317 :question/problem "The region $R$ is enclosed inside the cylinder $x^2+y^2=24$ for $x\\le 0$ and $y\\ge 0$, between the surfaces $z=x$ and $z=y^{2} + 8$. Express the following triple integral in cylindrical polar coordinates.\n\n$$\n\\iiint_R z \\left(x^{2} - y^{2}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}V\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/83/83886f6b8e5d21c89818cecb7266a3b01aafb4e215616c56383c9f9d52aa77bd.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is one-quarter of a right vertical cylinder of radius $2 \\sqrt{6}$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=x=r \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)},$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=y^{2} + 8=r^{2} \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 8.$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta,z):0\\le r\\le 2 \\sqrt{6},\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\le\\theta\\le \\pi,r \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\le z\\le r^{2} \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 8\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iiint_R z \\left(x^{2} - y^{2}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}V&=\\iiint_\\Delta z((r\\cos\\theta)^2-(r\\sin\\theta)^2)\\cdot r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\iiint_\\Delta rz(r^2\\cos^2\\theta-r^2\\sin^2\\theta)\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\iiint_\\Delta r^3z(\\cos^2\\theta-\\sin^2\\theta)\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\iiint_\\Delta r^3z\\cos(2\\theta)\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &={\\int }_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\pi}{\\int }_{0}^{2 \\sqrt{6}}{\\int }_{r \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}^{r^{2} \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 8}r^{3} z \\cos{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186556346 :question/problem "The region $R$ is enclosed inside the cylinder ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=5$ between the surfaces $z=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}$ and $z=4.$ Express the following triple integral in cylindrical polar coordinates. \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}V\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/f8/f869ef32fb741040d355c3280da541178bcac9b52477ebfa7d078d8b832a5903.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is a right vertical cylinder of radius $\\sqrt{5}$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}=r,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=4.$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta ,z)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le \\sqrt{5},\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,\\,r\\le z\\le 4\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}\\sqrt{(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}}\\cdot r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}r\\sqrt{{r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{5}}{\\int }_{r}^{4}{r}^{2}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186556350 :question/problem "The region $R$ is enclosed inside the cylinder $x^2+y^2=17$ for $x\\le 0$ and $y\\ge 0$, between the surfaces $z=x$ and $z=y^{2} + 12$. Express the following triple integral in cylindrical polar coordinates.\n\n$$\n\\iiint_R z \\left(x^{2} - y^{2}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}V\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/0e/0e609e4abfb4512377285af757fa4002f8e976352c72e54a808d156a781298b1.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is one-quarter of a right vertical cylinder of radius $\\sqrt{17}$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=x=r \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)},$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=y^{2} + 12=r^{2} \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 12.$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta,z):0\\le r\\le \\sqrt{17},\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\le\\theta\\le \\pi,r \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\le z\\le r^{2} \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 12\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iiint_R z \\left(x^{2} - y^{2}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}V&=\\iiint_\\Delta z((r\\cos\\theta)^2-(r\\sin\\theta)^2)\\cdot r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\iiint_\\Delta rz(r^2\\cos^2\\theta-r^2\\sin^2\\theta)\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\iiint_\\Delta r^3z(\\cos^2\\theta-\\sin^2\\theta)\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\iiint_\\Delta r^3z\\cos(2\\theta)\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &={\\int }_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\pi}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{17}}{\\int }_{r \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}^{r^{2} \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 12}r^{3} z \\cos{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186556353 :question/problem "![](images/aa/aa571435e7a44532cfb3bc61965b56b10e83c090236349851971e1b51c007ade.png)\n\nThe area of the triangle $△ABC$ is $\\frac{11}{2}\\,\\text{cm}^{2}.$ Use $\\sin30^{\\circ}=\\frac{1}{2}.$ Then, $AC=${{field-1}} $\\text{cm}.$" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the area of the triangle is \n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}bc\\operatorname{sin}⁡A,\n$$\n\nwhere $∠A$ is between the sides of lengths $b$ and $c.$ \n\nHere\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =AB=2\\text{ cm} \\\\ b & =AC \\\\ m∠A & ={30}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡{30}^{∘}=\\frac{1}{2},\n$$\n\nwe can calculate $b=AC,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot b\\cdot c\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡A \\\\ \\frac{11}{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot b\\cdot 2\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{30}^{∘} \\\\ \\frac{11}{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot b\\cdot 2\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\ \\frac{11}{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}b \\\\ b & =\\frac{\\frac{11}{2}}{\\frac{1}{2}} \\\\ b & =11\\text{ cm}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that $AC=11\\,\\text{cm}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186556359 :question/problem "![](images/76/76bdcef8f267043fe1f9c97f989ff3d6bfdf3b071158501cf43aef78976e93eb.png)\n\nThe area of the triangle $△ABC$ is $\\frac{1247\\sqrt{2}}{64}\\,\\text{cm}^{2}.$ Then, $AC=${{field-1}} $\\text{cm}.$" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the area of the triangle is \n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}bc\\operatorname{sin}⁡A,\n$$\n\nwhere $∠A$ is between the sides of lengths $b$ and $c.$ \n\nHere\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =AB=\\frac{43}{4}\\text{ cm} \\\\ b & =AC \\\\ m∠A & ={45}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡{45}^{∘}=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\n$$\n\nwe can calculate $b=AC,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot b\\cdot c\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡A \\\\ \\frac{1247\\sqrt{2}}{64} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot b\\cdot \\frac{43}{4}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{45}^{∘} \\\\ \\frac{1247\\sqrt{2}}{64} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot b\\cdot \\frac{43}{4}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right) \\\\ \\frac{1247\\sqrt{2}}{64} & =\\frac{43\\sqrt{2}}{16}b \\\\ b & =\\frac{\\frac{1247\\sqrt{2}}{64}}{\\frac{43\\sqrt{2}}{16}} \\\\ b & =\\frac{29}{4}\\text{ cm}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that $AC=\\frac{29}{4}\\,\\text{cm}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186556366 :question/problem "![](images/69/6949b198287b58d95ba47662a3432760d7e5a98d60bd006541269abcc9f3e2ab.png)\n\nThe area of the triangle $△ABC$ is $\\frac{21}{2}\\,\\text{mm}^{2}.$ Use $\\sin30^{\\circ}=\\frac{1}{2}.$ Find $AC.$" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the area of the triangle is \n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}bc\\operatorname{sin}⁡A,\n$$\n\nwhere $∠A$ is between the sides of lengths $b$ and $c.$ \n\nHere\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =AB=7\\text{ mm} \\\\ b & =AC \\\\ m∠A & ={30}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡{30}^{∘}=\\frac{1}{2},\n$$\n\nwe can calculate $b=AC,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot b\\cdot c\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡A \\\\ \\frac{21}{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot b\\cdot 7\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{30}^{∘} \\\\ \\frac{21}{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot b\\cdot 7\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\ \\frac{21}{2} & =\\frac{7}{4}b \\\\ b & =\\frac{\\frac{21}{2}}{\\frac{7}{4}} \\\\ b & =6\\text{ mm}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that $AC=6\\,\\text{mm}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186556379 :question/problem "![](images/52/52e6b64e78550f0e1cfeecc59a606921b52af85c457fff5e936f45a0a732c243.png)\n\nThe area of the triangle $△ABC$ is $1\\,\\text{cm}^{2}.$ Use $\\sin30^{\\circ}=\\frac{1}{2}.$ Then, $AC=${{field-1}} $\\text{cm}.$" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the area of the triangle is \n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}bc\\operatorname{sin}⁡A,\n$$\n\nwhere $∠A$ is between the sides of lengths $b$ and $c.$ \n\nHere\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =AB=2\\text{ cm} \\\\ b & =AC \\\\ m∠A & ={30}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡{30}^{∘}=\\frac{1}{2},\n$$\n\nwe can calculate $b=AC,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot b\\cdot c\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡A \\\\ 1 & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot b\\cdot 2\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{30}^{∘} \\\\ 1 & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot b\\cdot 2\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\ 1 & =\\frac{1}{2}b \\\\ b & =\\frac{1}{\\frac{1}{2}} \\\\ b & =2\\text{ cm}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that $AC=2\\,\\text{cm}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186556381 :question/problem "![](images/3f/3f14b28ecb2ab34328e461e66b6c00c5516b99d559e675db06306f804d5aaf29.png)\n\nThe area of the triangle $△ABC$ is $21\\,\\text{mm}^{2}.$ Use $\\sin30^{\\circ}=\\frac{1}{2}.$ Find $AC.$" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the area of the triangle is \n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}bc\\operatorname{sin}⁡A,\n$$\n\nwhere $∠A$ is between the sides of lengths $b$ and $c.$ \n\nHere\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =AB=7\\text{ mm} \\\\ b & =AC \\\\ m∠A & ={30}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡{30}^{∘}=\\frac{1}{2},\n$$\n\nwe can calculate $b=AC,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot b\\cdot c\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡A \\\\ 21 & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot b\\cdot 7\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{30}^{∘} \\\\ 21 & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot b\\cdot 7\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\ 21 & =\\frac{7}{4}b \\\\ b & =\\frac{21}{\\frac{7}{4}} \\\\ b & =12\\text{ mm}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that $AC=12\\,\\text{mm}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186556384 :question/problem "![](images/89/89a4b38c29ef11835af24fa0b98ee45a7f5fcbc6ee0a5fd0a3de4d0096144659.png)\n\nThe area of the triangle $△ABC$ is $\\frac{35\\sqrt{2}}{4}\\,\\text{cm}^{2}.$ Find $AC.$" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the area of the triangle is \n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}bc\\operatorname{sin}⁡A,\n$$\n\nwhere $∠A$ is between the sides of lengths $b$ and $c.$ \n\nHere\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =AB=\\frac{20}{3}\\text{ cm} \\\\ b & =AC \\\\ m∠A & ={135}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡{135}^{∘}=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\n$$\n\nwe can calculate $b=AC,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot b\\cdot c\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡A \\\\ \\frac{35\\sqrt{2}}{4} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot b\\cdot \\frac{20}{3}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{135}^{∘} \\\\ \\frac{35\\sqrt{2}}{4} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot b\\cdot \\frac{20}{3}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right) \\\\ \\frac{35\\sqrt{2}}{4} & =\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{3}b \\\\ b & =\\frac{\\frac{35\\sqrt{2}}{4}}{\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{3}} \\\\ b & =\\frac{21}{4}\\text{ cm}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that $AC=\\frac{21}{4}\\,\\text{cm}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186556391 :question/problem "![](images/27/272f91f05987c36217eeb9bcb74c4a7b9115f00dbf9358873616a09556803f4a.png)\n\nThe area of the triangle $△ABC$ is $\\frac{121\\sqrt{3}}{18}\\,\\text{cm}^{2}.$ Find $AC.$" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the area of the triangle is \n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}bc\\operatorname{sin}⁡A,\n$$\n\nwhere $∠A$ is between the sides of lengths $b$ and $c.$ \n\nHere\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =AB=\\frac{22}{3}\\text{ cm} \\\\ b & =AC \\\\ m∠A & ={120}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡{120}^{∘}=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\n$$\n\nwe can calculate $b=AC,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot b\\cdot c\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡A \\\\ \\frac{121\\sqrt{3}}{18} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot b\\cdot \\frac{22}{3}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{120}^{∘} \\\\ \\frac{121\\sqrt{3}}{18} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot b\\cdot \\frac{22}{3}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right) \\\\ \\frac{121\\sqrt{3}}{18} & =\\frac{11\\sqrt{3}}{6}b \\\\ b & =\\frac{\\frac{121\\sqrt{3}}{18}}{\\frac{11\\sqrt{3}}{6}} \\\\ b & =\\frac{11}{3}\\text{ cm}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that $AC=\\frac{11}{3}\\,\\text{cm}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186556396 :question/problem "![](images/3d/3dc502507d431d52979ff26a01089d61793a9487989d9203fd2f4f9246ecb1e3.png)\n\nThe area of the triangle $△ABC$ is $\\frac{111\\sqrt{2}}{32}\\,\\text{cm}^{2}.$ Then, $AC=${{field-1}} $\\text{cm}.$" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the area of the triangle is \n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}bc\\operatorname{sin}⁡A,\n$$\n\nwhere $∠A$ is between the sides of lengths $b$ and $c.$ \n\nHere\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =AB=\\frac{3}{2}\\text{ cm} \\\\ b & =AC \\\\ m∠A & ={45}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡{45}^{∘}=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\n$$\n\nwe can calculate $b=AC,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot b\\cdot c\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡A \\\\ \\frac{111\\sqrt{2}}{32} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot b\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{45}^{∘} \\\\ \\frac{111\\sqrt{2}}{32} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot b\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right) \\\\ \\frac{111\\sqrt{2}}{32} & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{8}b \\\\ b & =\\frac{\\frac{111\\sqrt{2}}{32}}{\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{8}} \\\\ b & =\\frac{37}{4}\\text{ cm}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that $AC=\\frac{37}{4}\\,\\text{cm}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186556400 :question/problem "![](images/77/7783777fcc75d7c12a8eaed0f703bc6a864e805fc8c814860a899e3737f71480.png)\n\nThe area of the triangle $△ABC$ is $\\frac{85\\sqrt{2}}{36}\\,\\text{mm}^{2}.$ Find $AC.$" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the area of the triangle is \n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}bc\\operatorname{sin}⁡A,\n$$\n\nwhere $∠A$ is between the sides of lengths $b$ and $c.$ \n\nHere\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =AB=\\frac{17}{3}\\text{ mm} \\\\ b & =AC \\\\ m∠A & ={45}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡{45}^{∘}=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\n$$\n\nwe can calculate $b=AC,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot b\\cdot c\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡A \\\\ \\frac{85\\sqrt{2}}{36} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot b\\cdot \\frac{17}{3}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{45}^{∘} \\\\ \\frac{85\\sqrt{2}}{36} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot b\\cdot \\frac{17}{3}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right) \\\\ \\frac{85\\sqrt{2}}{36} & =\\frac{17\\sqrt{2}}{12}b \\\\ b & =\\frac{\\frac{85\\sqrt{2}}{36}}{\\frac{17\\sqrt{2}}{12}} \\\\ b & =\\frac{5}{3}\\text{ mm}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that $AC=\\frac{5}{3}\\,\\text{mm}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186556401 :question/problem "![](images/6a/6a2da5468750823c21e259f6aa40a0c93342a2bebcc38a1292aa43686ca618fa.png)\n\nThe area of the triangle $△ABC$ is $\\frac{35}{4}\\,\\text{cm}^{2}.$ Use $\\sin30^{\\circ}=\\frac{1}{2}.$ Find $AC.$" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the area of the triangle is \n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}bc\\operatorname{sin}⁡A,\n$$\n\nwhere $∠A$ is between the sides of lengths $b$ and $c.$ \n\nHere\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =AB=7\\text{ cm} \\\\ b & =AC \\\\ m∠A & ={30}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡{30}^{∘}=\\frac{1}{2},\n$$\n\nwe can calculate $b=AC,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot b\\cdot c\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡A \\\\ \\frac{35}{4} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot b\\cdot 7\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{30}^{∘} \\\\ \\frac{35}{4} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot b\\cdot 7\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\ \\frac{35}{4} & =\\frac{7}{4}b \\\\ b & =\\frac{\\frac{35}{4}}{\\frac{7}{4}} \\\\ b & =5\\text{ cm}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that $AC=5\\,\\text{cm}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557214 :question/problem "Given that $f(x)={(2x-11)}^{6},$ find ${f}^{′}(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $g(x)=2x-11$ and $f(g(x))=g(x)^{6}.$ \n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(g(x)) & =({g}^{6})^{′}=6{g}^{5}, \\\\ {g}^{′}(x) & =(2x-11)^{′}=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the chain rule, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={f}^{′}(g(x))\\cdot {g}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =6{g}^{5}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =12(2x-11)^{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557238 :question/problem "Let $S$ be the boundary of the rectangular prism enclosed by the planes $x=0,$ $x=3,$ $y=-1,$ $y=2,$ $z=0,$ $z=4.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $\\mathbf{F}=y{e}^{x}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+{z}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+3z\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k},$ the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $S$ can be written as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S}={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{4}\\hspace{0.167em}\\bbox[2pt,border: 1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AAAAA}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}z\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}y\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing part of the expression?" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S}={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $\\mathbf{F},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y{e}^{x})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({z}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(3z) \\\\  & =y{e}^{x}+0+3 \\\\  & =y{e}^{x}+3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid region enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∭}_{R}(y{e}^{x}+3)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{4}\\hspace{0.167em}\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}y{e}^{x}+3}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}z\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}y\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557259 :question/problem "Find the slope of the tangent to the curve $y=\\sqrt{2x+1}$ at the point where $x=4.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the slope at a given point, we have to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point.\n\nWe use the chain rule in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet $u=2x+1.$ Then $y=\\sqrt{u}.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the two together gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2x+1}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $x=4,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=4} & ={\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2x+1}}\\right)|}_{x=4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2(4)+1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557290 :question/problem "Let $S$ be the boundary of the rectangular prism enclosed by the planes $x=0,$ $x=2,$ $y=1,$ $y=3,$ $z=1,$ $z=2.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $\\mathbf{F}=x\\sqrt{y}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-\\operatorname{ln}y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+xy\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k},$ the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $S$ can be written as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S}={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{3}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\bbox[2pt,border: 1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AAAAA}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}z\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}y\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing part of the expression?" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S}={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $\\mathbf{F},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x\\sqrt{y})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-\\operatorname{ln}y)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(xy) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{y}-\\frac{1}{y}+0 \\\\  & =\\sqrt{y}-\\frac{1}{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid region enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∭}_{R}(\\sqrt{y}-\\frac{1}{y})\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{3}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}\\sqrt{y}-\\frac{1}{y}}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}z\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}y\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557293 :question/problem "![](images/0b/0b460a07026e489fb7c63dad99c04c7e44540138632626dcd3ebcafb5c0a93d3.png)\n\nThe area of $ABCD$ is $52{\\text{ in}}^{2}.$   What is the area of $△BCD$ rounded to the nearest integer?" :question/worked-solution "Let's start by finding the area of $△ABD.$ We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{△ABD} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot AB\\cdot AD\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡A \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 9\\cdot 16\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{66}^{∘} \\\\  & =72\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{66}^{∘} \\\\  & =65.775… \\\\  & ≈66{\\text{ in}}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer.\n\nThe difference of the areas of  $△ABD$ and the quadrilateral $ABCD$ gives the area of $△BCD.$ Therefore, \n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{△BCD} & ={A}_{△ABD}-{A}_{ABCD} \\\\  & =66-52 \\\\  & =14\\,{\\text{ in}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557301 :question/problem "Given that $f(x)={(6x-5)}^{6},$ find ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n${f}^{′}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $g(x)=6x-5$ and $f(g(x))=g(x)^{6}.$ \n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(g(x)) & =({g}^{6})^{′}=6{g}^{5}, \\\\ {g}^{′}(x) & =(6x-5)^{′}=6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the chain rule, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={f}^{′}(g(x))\\cdot {g}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =6{g}^{5}\\cdot (6) \\\\  & =36(6x-5)^{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557305 :question/problem "![](images/27/27dc9ff8452d9a9aa95322492549ba0eabed5f68a7aa2e586ebe239143268bde.png)\n\nRounded to the nearest integer, what is the area of the quadrilateral $ABCD?$" :question/worked-solution "The area of the quadrilateral $ABCD$ is the sum of the areas of the triangles $△ABD$ and $△BDC$ (see the illustration below).\n\n![](images/da/da7cc00005fbeb686443e2075009fda7c68f6d379a8ddb924a7dfe5b60e6ab16.png)\n\n \n\nFirst we find the area for $△ABD:$ \n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{ABD} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 10\\cdot 8\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{75}^{∘} \\\\  & =40\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{75}^{∘} \\\\  & ≈38.637,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.\n\nNow we find the area for $△BCD:$ \n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{BDC} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 5\\cdot 12\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{67.2}^{∘} \\\\  & =30\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{67.2}^{∘} \\\\  & ≈27.656,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.\n\nFinally, we add together the areas of our two triangles to determine the area of $ABCD:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{ABCD} & ={A}_{ABD}+{A}_{BDC} \\\\  & =38.637+27.656 \\\\  & =66.293\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, to the nearest integer, the area of the quadrilateral $ABCD$ is $66.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557319 :question/problem "Let $S$ be the boundary of the finite solid region enclosed by the planes $y=-1,$ $y=1,$ $z=0,$ $z=2,$ and the surfaces $x=y-z$ and $x=1+{y}^{2}.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $\\mathbf{F}=\\mathbf{i}+x{z}^{3}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+xz\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k},$ the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $S$ can be written as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S}=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(1)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{z}^{3})+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(xz) \\\\  & =0+0+x \\\\  & =x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}x\\,\\text{d}V,\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is a type II solid region that can be written as\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,-1\\le y\\le 1,\\,0\\le z\\le 2,\\,y-z\\le x\\le 1+{y}^{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}x\\,\\text{d}V & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{y-z}^{1+{y}^{2}}\\,x\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557447 :question/problem "Use a change of variables to calculate the area of the finite region $D$ in the first quadrant bounded by the following curves and lines.\n$$\n\\displaystyle y = \\frac{3}{x}, y = \\frac{7}{x}, y = 4x, y = 5x\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe'll assume this transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv,\n$$\n\n  \n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in five steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nNotice that we can rewrite the equations of our curves as follows:\n\n$$\nxy=3,\\,xy=7,\\,\\frac{y}{x}=4,\\,\\frac{y}{x}=5\n$$\n\nThis suggests the following change of variables:\n\n$$\nu=xy,\\,v=\\frac{y}{x}\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nWe substitute $u=xy$ into the equations of the first and second curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & xy=3\\,⟹\\,u=3 \\\\  & xy=7\\,⟹\\,u=7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we substitute $v=\\frac{y}{x}$ into the equations of the third and fourth curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\frac{y}{x}=4\\,⟹\\,v=4 \\\\  & \\frac{y}{x}=5\\,⟹\\,v=5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,3\\le u\\le 7,\\,4\\le v\\le 5\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/3d/3dc524083463c8c370ff1f79d68093f0d3f2c2fe1ceb5cd47824782e35a0bac9.png)\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}y & x \\\\ -\\frac{y}{x^2} & \\frac{1}{x}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{y}{x}+\\frac{y}{x} \\\\  & =2v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{2v}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{1}{2v}\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{3}^{7}\\,\\text{d}u{\\int }_{4}^{5}\\frac{1}{v}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (7-3)\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡5-\\operatorname{ln}⁡4) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\frac{5}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557450 :question/problem "Use a change of variables to calculate the area of the finite region $D$ in the first quadrant bounded by the following curves and lines.\n$$\n\\displaystyle y = \\frac{2}{x}, y = \\frac{5}{x}, y = 2x, y = 3x\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe'll assume this transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv,\n$$\n\n  \n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in five steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nNotice that we can rewrite the equations of our curves as follows:\n\n$$\nxy=2,\\,xy=5,\\,\\frac{y}{x}=2,\\,\\frac{y}{x}=3\n$$\n\nThis suggests the following change of variables:\n\n$$\nu=xy,\\,v=\\frac{y}{x}\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nWe substitute $u=xy$ into the equations of the first and second curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & xy=2\\,⟹\\,u=2 \\\\  & xy=5\\,⟹\\,u=5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we substitute $v=\\frac{y}{x}$ into the equations of the third and fourth curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\frac{y}{x}=2\\,⟹\\,v=2 \\\\  & \\frac{y}{x}=3\\,⟹\\,v=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,2\\le u\\le 5,\\,2\\le v\\le 3\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/2c/2cdd0431c256b72ded23c14fac4098b518a46603d46d5dfd238c54b3a46c66db.png)\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}y & x \\\\ -\\frac{y}{x^2} & \\frac{1}{x}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{y}{x}+\\frac{y}{x} \\\\  & =2v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{2v}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{1}{2v}\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{2}^{5}\\,\\text{d}u{\\int }_{2}^{3}\\frac{1}{v}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (5-2)\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557452 :question/problem "Use a change of variables to calculate the area of the finite region $D$ in the first quadrant bounded by the following curves and lines.\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{2}{x},\\,y=\\frac{5}{x},\\,x=2{y}^{2},\\,x=3{y}^{2}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe'll assume this transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv,\n$$\n\n  \n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in five steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nNotice that we can rewrite the equations of our curves as follows:\n\n$$\nxy=2,\\,xy=5,\\,\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}=2,\\,\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}=3\n$$\n\nThis suggests the following change of variables:\n\n$$\nu=xy,\\,v=\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nWe substitute $u=xy$ into the equations of the first and second curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & xy=2\\,⟹\\,u=2 \\\\  & xy=5\\,⟹\\,u=5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we substitute $v=\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}$ into the equations of the third and fourth curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}=2\\,⟹\\,v=2 \\\\  & \\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}=3\\,⟹\\,v=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,2\\le u\\le 5,\\,2\\le v\\le 3\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/2c/2cdd0431c256b72ded23c14fac4098b518a46603d46d5dfd238c54b3a46c66db.png)\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}y & x \\\\ \\frac{1}{{y}^{2}} & -\\frac{2x}{{y}^{3}}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2x}{{y}^{2}}-\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3x}{{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =-3v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{3v}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|-\\frac{1}{3v}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{∬}_{Δ}\\frac{1}{v}\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{2}^{5}\\,\\text{d}u{\\int }_{2}^{3}\\frac{1}{v}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (5-2)\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557458 :question/problem "Use a change of variables to calculate the area of the finite region $D$ in the first quadrant bounded by the following curves and lines.\n$$\n\\displaystyle y = \\frac{1}{x}, y = \\frac{4}{x}, y = 3x, y = 4x\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe'll assume this transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv,\n$$\n\n  \n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in five steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nNotice that we can rewrite the equations of our curves as follows:\n\n$$\nxy=1,\\,xy=4,\\,\\frac{y}{x}=3,\\,\\frac{y}{x}=4\n$$\n\nThis suggests the following change of variables:\n\n$$\nu=xy,\\,v=\\frac{y}{x}\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nWe substitute $u=xy$ into the equations of the first and second curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & xy=1\\,⟹\\,u=1 \\\\  & xy=4\\,⟹\\,u=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we substitute $v=\\frac{y}{x}$ into the equations of the third and fourth curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\frac{y}{x}=3\\,⟹\\,v=3 \\\\  & \\frac{y}{x}=4\\,⟹\\,v=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 4,\\,3\\le v\\le 4\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/88/88750e49134feaa087b1f48ddee12faeb30ca8a31dd3b5bebb989f4c7c891ae7.png)\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}y & x \\\\ -\\frac{y}{x^2} & \\frac{1}{x}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{y}{x}+\\frac{y}{x} \\\\  & =2v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{2v}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{1}{2v}\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\,\\text{d}u{\\int }_{3}^{4}\\frac{1}{v}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (4-1)\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
